    {
      "index": 4,
      "id": "contribution",
      "heading": "Contribution",
      "chunks": [
        {
          "chunk_index": 1,
          "chunk_id": "s04_c01",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s04_c1_core_contribution_general_dual_based",
          "text": "The core contribution is a general dual-based formulation of Kernel PCA built on the dualization of a difference of convex functions. This formulation does two things at once.",
          "expected_role": "content",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "core",
            "contribution",
            "general",
            "dual-based",
            "formulation",
            "kernel",
            "pca",
            "built",
            "dualization",
            "difference"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "chunk_index": 2,
          "chunk_id": "s04_c02",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s04_c2_first_turns_problem_one_over",
          "text": "First, it turns the problem into one over a finite-dimensional dual matrix, which can be solved with efficient gradient-based methods and avoids the expensive singular value decomposition, even when the feature space is infinite-dimensional.",
          "expected_role": "method",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "first",
            "turns",
            "problem",
            "one",
            "over",
            "finite-dimensional",
            "dual",
            "matrix",
            "which",
            "solved"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "chunk_index": 3,
          "chunk_id": "s04_c03",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s04_c3_second_makes_objective_modular_choos",
          "text": "Second, it makes the objective modular: by choosing objectives expressible as Moreau envelopes, the same framework promotes robustness or sparsity without leaving the dual picture.",
          "expected_role": "guidance",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "second",
            "makes",
            "objective",
            "modular",
            "choosing",
            "objectives",
            "expressible",
            "moreau",
            "envelopes",
            "same"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "chunk_index": 4,
          "chunk_id": "s04_c04",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s04_c4_authors_supply_supporting_theory_con",
          "text": "The authors supply the supporting theory, concrete L-BFGS and difference-of-convex algorithms, and the proximal operators needed to make the robust and sparse cases practical.",
          "expected_role": "content",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "authors",
            "supply",
            "supporting",
            "theory",
            "concrete",
            "l-bfgs",
            "difference-of-convex",
            "algorithms",
            "proximal",
            "operators"
          ],
          "required": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "index": 5,
      "id": "method",
      "heading": "Method",
      "chunks": [
        {
          "chunk_index": 1,
          "chunk_id": "s05_c01",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s05_c1_method_starts_writing_kernel_pca",
          "text": "The method starts by writing Kernel PCA as the minimization of a difference of two convex functions over the Stiefel manifold of orthonormal frames.",
          "expected_role": "method",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "method",
            "starts",
            "writing",
            "kernel",
            "pca",
            "minimization",
            "difference",
            "two",
            "convex",
            "functions"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "chunk_index": 2,
          "chunk_id": "s05_c02",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s05_c2_general_dualization_proposition_conv",
          "text": "A general dualization proposition then converts this primal problem, whose variables live in a possibly infinite-dimensional feature space, into a dual problem whose variable H is a finite n-by-s matrix, perfectly suited to gradient descent.",
          "expected_role": "content",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "general",
            "dualization",
            "proposition",
            "converts",
            "primal",
            "problem",
            "whose",
            "variables",
            "live",
            "possibly"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "chunk_index": 3,
          "chunk_id": "s05_c03",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s05_c3_indicator_function_stiefel_manifold",
          "text": "The indicator function of the Stiefel manifold turns, in the dual, into a nuclear-norm term the authors call pi of H, equal to the trace of the square root of H-transpose G H. Its gradient only requires the singular value decomposition of a small s-by-s matrix, not the full n-by-n Gram matrix, which is the source of the speedup. For the standard problem they run L-BFGS.",
          "expected_role": "content",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "indicator",
            "function",
            "stiefel",
            "manifold",
            "turns",
            "dual",
            "nuclear-norm",
            "term",
            "authors",
            "call"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "chunk_index": 4,
          "chunk_id": "s05_c04",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s05_c4_promote_robustness_sparsity_they_add",
          "text": "To promote robustness or sparsity, they add a Moreau-envelope term to the objective and optimize with a difference-of-convex algorithm, plugging in closed-form proximal operators for the Huber and epsilon-insensitive losses.",
          "expected_role": "figure",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "promote",
            "robustness",
            "sparsity",
            "they",
            "add",
            "moreau-envelope",
            "term",
            "objective",
            "optimize",
            "difference-of-convex"
          ],
          "required": true
        }
      ]
    },
